<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Treasury_API (Named Credential)
    Pillar V.1 / App. C.2 -- the sanctioned secret-storage primitive for the
    Treasury integration endpoint. Carries no plaintext secret; secret material
    lives in the certificate referenced by the External Credential / authProvider.

    This is a legacy-style NamedCredential (single-step). The modern pattern is
    NamedCredential + ExternalCredential, where the credential lives in the
    ExternalCredential and per-user named principals are supported. Both are
    valid; the choice depends on per-user vs. shared service-account semantics.

    CERTIFICATE ROTATION & 2026 COMPLIANCE:
    - Certificate lifespan: Max 200 days (as of March 15, 2026); plan quarterly rotation minimum
    - Further reductions: 100 days by March 2027; 47 days by March 2029 (plan monthly rotation by 2028)
    - DigiCert Global Root G2: Verify all downstream systems trust this root (Feb 5, 2026 transition)
    - Dual-use cert ban: If using mTLS, verify client & server certificates are SEPARATE after June 15, 2026
      (Chrome will reject dual-use certs used for both server and client auth simultaneously)
    - See Pillar 5.3 (2026 Certificate Transitions) and Appendix H (cert rotation runbook)

    NOT official Salesforce documentation. Validate the NamedCredential schema
    (specifically the protocol enum and certificate reference style) against
    the current Metadata API Developer Guide.
-->
<NamedCredential xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <allowMergeFieldsInBody>false</allowMergeFieldsInBody>
    <allowMergeFieldsInHeader>true</allowMergeFieldsInHeader>
    <calloutStatus>Enabled</calloutStatus>
    <endpoint>https://treasury.acme.example.com</endpoint>
    <generateAuthorizationHeader>true</generateAuthorizationHeader>
    <label>Treasury API</label>
    <namedCredentialType>Legacy</namedCredentialType>
    <principalType>NamedUser</principalType>
    <protocol>Oauth</protocol>
</NamedCredential>
<!--
    Schema note: the current Salesforce NamedCredential schema requires
    <namedCredentialType> with values "Legacy" or "SecuredEndpoint". This file
    keeps the legacy all-in-one shape (auth + endpoint together) so the
    pedagogical contrast with the External Credential pair is clear.

    Modern pattern (recommended for new integrations):
      <namedCredentialType>SecuredEndpoint</namedCredentialType>
      <externalCredential>Treasury_OAuth</externalCredential>
      (drop <protocol>, <principalType>, <generateAuthorizationHeader>)
    See:
    https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=xcloud.nc_named_creds_and_ext_creds.htm
    https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.api_meta.meta/api_meta/meta_namedcredential.htm
-->
